For the really simple route, set your secret:

export SEALED_OWNER_SECRET='your-long-local-secret'

Then black-box basically any file, image, backup, disk image, or folder:

python SEALED_BLACK_BOX_v2_2.py snapshot /path/to/item

Or give it a label:

python SEALED_BLACK_BOX_v2_2.py snapshot ~/Pictures --label "Pictures backup"

See what is sealed:

python SEALED_BLACK_BOX_v2_2.py list

Restore a single file:

python SEALED_BLACK_BOX_v2_2.py unseal OBJECT_ID --out restored.jpg

Restore a folder:

python SEALED_BLACK_BOX_v2_2.py restore-dir OBJECT_ID /path/to/restore

For a computer backup/disk image, it works the same way:

python SEALED_BLACK_BOX_v2_2.py snapshot /backups/my-laptop.img --label "Laptop image"

That can be a very large file because the vault encrypts it in chunks rather than trying to load the entire machine image into RAM.

For the live operating system itself, I’d still use FileVault/BitLocker/LUKS underneath SEALED. Then the stack becomes:

live computer
    ↓
full-disk encryption
    ↓
SEALED black box
    ├── photos/images
    ├── documents
    ├── source code
    ├── folders
    ├── archives
    ├── backups
    └── whole-machine disk images

One tiny truthfulness fix also went in while testing: when you seal a directory, its encrypted record now remembers the original directory path, rather than accidentally recording the temporary .tar.gz staging path.

